arseIntegerList(String input) {\n        List<Integer> integerList = new ArrayList<>();\n        String[] tokens = input.split(\",\");\n        for (String token : tokens) {\n            try {\n                int value = Integer.parseInt(token.trim());\n                integerList.add(value);\n            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n                // Ignore invalid input\n            }\n        }\n        return integerList;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis program first prompts the user to enter a comma-separated list of integers, which is then parsed into a `List<Integer>` using the `parseIntegerList` method. The program also prompts the user to enter a target value.\n\nThe algorithm to count the number of values less than the target value is implemented using a simple for loop. For each integer in the list, if the integer is less than the target value, the count is incremented.\n\nThe program also includes error handling for invalid input or malformed data. If the user enters an invalid integer, it is ignored.\n\nTo test the program, you can input various lists of integers and target values and verify that the output is correct. For example, inputting the list `1, 2, 3, 4, 5` and the target value `3` should output `Number of values less than 3: 2`.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nThis Java program looks great so far! Can you give me some more examples of how to test this program with different lists of intege","tokens":["<","|","im","_","start","|>","system","\n","You"," are"," a"," helpful"," assistant",".<","|","im","_","end","|>","\n","<","|","im","_","start","|>","user","\n","In"," designing"," the"," Java"," program",","," utilize"," an"," efficient"," algorithm"," to"," traverse"," the"," list"," of"," integers"," and"," count"," the"," number"," of"," values"," less"," than"," the"," target"," value","."," Use"," a"," user","-","friendly"," interface"," to"," prompt"," the"," user"," to"," input"," the"," list"," of"," integers"," and"," the"," target"," val